{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q003_actin_like_atpase_divergence","answer":"SCOPe places the catalytic (ATPase/kinase) domains of actin/HSP70, hexokinase and glycerol kinase in the same superfamily, the Actin-like ATPase domain (c.55.1). It is in fold c.55 (ribonuclease H-like motif), class c (alpha/beta). Within that superfamily they fall into different families: actin and HSP70 are in actin/HSP70-type families, while hexokinase and glycerol kinase are in separate sugar-kinase-type families. I am recalling the family-level c.55.1.x numbering from memory and have not checked it against the current release, so I give only the superfamily and fold identifiers with confidence. The relationship is therefore remote homology, meaning a common evolutionary origin supported by structure and conserved ATP-binding features. It is not close, family-level relatedness, and sequence identity between the families is low.

What it permits you to infer: (1) Common ancestry. These domains diverged from a shared ancestral ATP-binding domain, even though their roles are very different (cytoskeletal polymerization, chaperone activity, sugar phosphorylation, glycerol phosphorylation). (2) A shared architecture: two similar subdomains (I and II), each with an RNase H-like core, forming a deep interdomain cleft that binds nucleotide and a divalent cation. Binding of ATP typically goes with domain closure, so you can expect a conserved general ATP-binding and phosphoryl-transfer framework and similar overall conformational behavior. (3) A reasonable basis for structural comparison and for cautious, fold-level modeling of the core.

What it does not permit: you cannot transfer specific function, substrate specificity, catalytic residues, regulation, oligomerization or cellular role across families. Actin is not a kinase, HSP70 is a chaperone, and the two kinases use different substrates. Superfamily membership does not mean the same reaction. A single sequence-based template across these families would give unreliable loop, cleft and substrate-site models, and alignment-based residue mapping is uncertain at this low identity. The fold-level grouping (c.55) is also broader than the superfamily. The RNase H-like fold contains other superfamilies whose relationship is only structural similarity, not demonstrated homology, so the evidence for homology here depends specifically on the superfamily assignment. For any functional claim you need family-level or direct evidence, such as close sequence homology, conserved active-site residues, or experimental data.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
